// Rayleigh-Taylor instability at late time. A heavy fluid (mat 2) lies over
// a light one (mat 1) in a box one wavelength wide, 1 x 2, with reflecting
// side walls, and a single-mode perturbation has grown into a spike of heavy
// fluid falling through the light and two half bubbles of light fluid rising
// along the walls. Shear at the spike's head has rolled its edges up into
// rims that curl back toward the stem, each trapping a pocket of light fluid
// under it. This mushroom is what every multi-material hydrodynamics code
// draws on its way to a turbulent mixing layer, and it is the configuration
// in which an interface-conforming mesh has to be rebuilt.
//
// What this domain is for. One interface, with no junction anywhere on it,
// that is as hard as an interface gets while staying resolvable. Its tangent
// turns through +-345 degrees between the stem and the tips of the rims; it
// overhangs itself on both sides, so it is a graph over neither axis; and
// its two sides come within 0.069 of each other in the rims (3.5 elements at
// NP = 100, h = 0.02 here). A field aligned to it has to turn almost a full
// circle inside each rim, and a layout has to fit blocks into the pockets,
// the rims and the neck at once. The interface meets both walls at right
// angles (they are symmetry planes), and the whole is mirror-symmetric about
// x = 0.5, as a single-mode instability is.
//
// The interface is one Spline through samples 0.02 apart of a centripetal
// Catmull-Rom curve through hand-placed control points.

